 records. <source>J Clin Epidemiol</source><year>2007</year>;<volume>60</volume>:<fpage>1190</fpage>-4.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17938062</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ref31"><label>31</label><citation citation-type="journal">Banks E, Beral V, Cameron R, Hogg A, Langley N, Barnes I, et al. Agreement between general practice prescription data and self-reported use of hormone replacement therapy and treatment for various illnesses. <source>J Epidemiol Biostat</source><year>2001</year>;<volume>6</volume>:<fpage>357</fpage>-63.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12036270</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ref32"><label>32</label><citation citation-type="journal">Watson J, Wise L, Green J. Prescribing of hormone therapy for the menopause, tibolone, and bisphosphonates in women in the UK between 1991 and 2005. <source>Eur J Clin Pharmacol</source><year>2007</year>;<volume>63</volume>:<fpage>843</fpage>-9.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17598097</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><notes notes-type="citation-text"><p><bold>Cite this as:</bold> <italic>BMJ</italic> 2008;337:a386</p></notes></back></article>